.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt DATE 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm date
.Nd display or set the date and time
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl jnRu
.Op Fl I Ns Op Ar precision
.Op Fl r Ar seconds | Fl r Ar file
.Oo Fl v Oo Cm + | Cm - Oc Ns Ar val Ns Oo Cm y | m | w | d | H | M | S Oc Oc ...
.Op Cm + Ns Ar output_fmt
.Nm
.Op Fl jnu
.Sm off
.Oo Oo Oo Oo Oo Ar cc Oc Ar yy Oc Ar mm Oc Ar dd Oc Ar HH Oc Ar MM Op Cm \&. Ar SS
.Sm on
.Op Cm + Ns Ar output_fmt
.Nm
.Op Fl jnRu
.Fl f Ar input_fmt new_date
.Op Cm + Ns Ar output_fmt
.Sh DESCRIPTION
With no operand,
.Nm
writes the current date and time.
Given
.Cm + Ns Ar output_fmt ,
it writes them in that format, as
.Xr strftime 3
takes it; otherwise as
.Ql %a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y .
.Pp
An operand of digits sets the system clock, which only the super-user may do:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact -offset indent
.It Ar cc
century, the first two digits of the year
.It Ar yy
year in the century; without
.Ar cc ,
69 to 99 are 1969 to 1999 and 00 to 68 are 2000 to 2068
.It Ar mm
month, 01 to 12
.It Ar dd
day of the month
.It Ar HH
hour, 00 to 23
.It Ar MM
minute
.It Ar SS
second
.El
Fields left out keep their present values, but seconds become 0.
Afterwards the new time is written.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar input_fmt
Read the operand with
.Xr strptime 3
and
.Ar input_fmt
instead; fields it doesn't set keep their present values.
.It Fl I Ns Op Ar precision
Write the time in ISO 8601 form, to the
.Cm date
(the default),
.Cm hours ,
.Cm minutes
or
.Cm seconds ,
with the offset from UTC.
.It Fl j
Don't set the clock: only write the time the operand names.
With
.Fl f ,
a way to convert dates from one format to another.
.It Fl n
Accepted for compatibility; it changes nothing.
.It Fl R
Write the time as RFC 2822 has it:
.Ql %a, %d %b %Y \&%T %z .
.It Fl r Ar seconds
Use this time, in seconds since the epoch, instead of now.
.It Fl r Ar file
Use the time
.Ar file
was last modified.
.It Fl u
Work in UTC, not the local time zone.
.It Fl v Oo Cm + | Cm - Oc Ns Ar val Ns Ar unit
Adjust the time before writing it.
Without a sign the field is set to
.Ar val ,
with one it is moved forward or back by
.Ar val .
The units are
.Cm y
(years),
.Cm m
(months, 1 to 12),
.Cm w
(weeks; without a sign, a day of the week, 0 to 6 from Sunday),
.Cm d
(days),
.Cm H
(hours),
.Cm M
(minutes) and
.Cm S
(seconds).
A month or weekday may be named instead, as
.Ql -v+fri
for the next Friday or
.Ql -v-jan
for the last January; a named day or month equal to the present one moves
nothing.
A day past the end of a month after a change of month is brought back to its
last day.
Adjustments in hours or smaller are of elapsed time; larger ones keep the
time of day across a change of daylight saving time.
Several
.Fl v
options apply in the order given.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TZ
.It Ev TZ
The time zone, for times shown and set.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Seconds since the epoch:
.Dl $ date +%s
.Pp
The last day of this month:
.Dl $ date -v1d -v+1m -v-1d +%F
.Pp
Set the clock to 8:30 this morning:
.Dl # date 0830
.Pp
Convert a date to another form without setting anything:
.Dl $ date -j -f %Y-%m-%d 2026-12-25 +%A
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clock_settime 2 ,
.Xr strftime 3 ,
.Xr strptime 3 ,
.Xr adjkerntz 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq)
but for how a time to set is written: POSIX's
.Ar mmddhhmm Ns Op Ar [cc]yy
is the BSDs'
.Ar [[[[[cc]yy]mm]dd]HH]MM
here, as in all three of them.
The options but
.Fl u
are extensions, as in the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
